rect**.\n\nHere is the analysis against the required criteria:\n\n---\n\n**1) Formal awards ceremony setting with stage and audience**  \n✅ Confirmed. Multiple frames (e.g., 5, 6, 23) show a large auditorium with a formal stage, red curtains, golden lighting, and a seated audience in formal wear. This matches the Oscars setting.\n\n**2) Oscar statuette awards being presented**  \n✅ Confirmed. Frame 9–11 show Brad Pitt holding an Oscar statuette while giving a speech. Frame 26–28 show Bong Joon-ho on stage with an Oscar statuette. This confirms award presentations.\n\n**3) Celebrities in formal attire (tuxedos, gowns)**  \n✅ Confirmed. Brad Pitt (frames 9–11), Quentin Tarantino and others in the audience (frames 1, 4), Keanu Reeves and Diane Keaton (frames 14–22), and Bong Joon-ho (frames 24–30) are all in formal wear — tuxedos, gowns, or elegant suits.\n\n**4) Presenters announcing winners and giving speeches**  \n✅ Confirmed. Brad Pitt (9–11) is giving an acceptance speech. Bong Joon-ho (24–30) is speaking at the podium. Keanu Reeves and Diane Keaton (14–22) appear to be presenting or speaking together on stage.\n\n**5) Performances or musical numbers on stage**  \n❌ Not observed in these 30 frames. While the global summary mentions performances, none are visible in this specific set of frames. This doesn’t disprove the 2020 Oscars, but it’s a gap in evidence.\n\n**6) Behind-the-scenes moments**  \n❌ Not observed. All frames show on-stage or audience shots — no backstage, dressing room, or candid prep moments are visible.\n\n---\n\n**Crucial Point: Specific Evidence of the 2020 Oscars**\n\nWhile the frames depict a legitimate Oscars ceremony, **there is no on-screen text, logo, or year indicator** (e.g., “92nd Academy Awards,” “2020,” or “Oscars 2020”) visible in any of the 30 frames to confirm this is specifically the **2020** ceremony.\n\nHowever, **contextual evidence strongly suggests it is the 2020 Oscars**:\n\n- **Brad Pitt** won Best Supporting Actor for *Once Upon a Time in Hollywood* at the 2020 Oscars (awarded Feb 9, 2020).\n- **Bong Joon-ho** won Best Director and Best Picture for *Parasite* at the same ceremony — his speech and appearance (frames 24–30) match that historic moment.\n- **Keanu Reeves and Diane Keaton** presented together at the 2020 Oscars (frames 14–22).\n- The **ABC logo** is visible in multiple frames (e.g., 1, 5, 9, 14, 23, 26), which is consistent with the 2020 broadcast.\n\n---\n\n**Conclusion:**\n\nWhile the frames **do depict the 2020 Oscars ceremony**, the answer **“The entire video narrates the complete process of the 2020 Oscars ceremony”** is **not verifiable from these 30 frames alone**.\n\nThe frames only show **selected moments** — acceptance speeches by Brad Pitt and Bong Joon-ho, and a presenting segment with Keanu Reeves and Diane Keaton. There is **no evidence** in these frames of the *complete process* — no opening, no full list of categories, no closing, no musical performances, no backstage moments.\n\nFurthermore, the global summary mentions a *compilation* of *various* Oscars ceremonies, and while these 30 frames are from 2020, we cannot confirm from this subset that the *entire video* is exclusively the 2020 ceremony.\n\n---\n\n✅ **Final Verdict: Answer '1' is INCORRECT.**\n\nThe frames show *parts* of the 2020 Oscars, but not the *complete process*, and without broader context, we cannot confirm the *entire video* is solely the 2020 ceremony. The global summary already suggests it’s a compilation, which contradicts answer '1'."}, {'batch_start': 30, 'batch_end': 60, 'image_paths': ['/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/-WnyRMZqV1U/frames/frame_0828.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/-WnyRMZqV1U/frames/frame_0829.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/-WnyRMZqV1U/frames/frame_0830.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/-WnyRMZqV1U/frames/frame_1144.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/-WnyRMZqV1U/frames/frame_1145.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbe