te process of the 2020 Oscars ceremony” — is NOT correct.**\n\nHere’s the detailed verification against the required criteria:\n\n---\n\n### 1) **Formal awards ceremony setting with stage and audience**\n✅ **Confirmed.**  \nFrames 9–10 show a wide shot of the Oscars stage with the “LIVE ACTION SHORT FILM” category displayed on a large archway, the Oscar statuette illuminated center stage, and a full audience in the theater. The lighting, stage design, and audience seating are consistent with a formal awards ceremony.\n\n---\n\n### 2) **Oscar statuette awards being presented**\n✅ **Confirmed.**  \nFrames 1–3 show Bong Joon-ho holding the Oscar statuette on stage. Frames 15, 18–20, and 23–25 show another winner (producer Peter Farrelly) holding the Oscar statuette while giving a speech. This confirms physical presentation of the award.\n\n---\n\n### 3) **Celebrities in formal attire (tuxedos, gowns)**\n✅ **Confirmed.**  \nMultiple frames show men in tuxedos (e.g., Bong Joon-ho in Frame 1, presenters in Frames 13–14, Peter Farrelly in Frame 15) and women in gowns (e.g., the woman beside Farrelly in Frame 15). This aligns with Oscars dress code.\n\n---\n\n### 4) **Presenters announcing winners and giving speeches**\n✅ **Confirmed.**  \nFrames 1–3, 15, 18–25, and 26–30 show winners speaking at the podium. Frames 13–14 show presenters (Shia LaBeouf and another man) reading from an envelope — a classic Oscars moment. The presence of microphones and podiums confirms speeches and announcements.\n\n---\n\n### 5) **Performances or musical numbers on stage**\n❌ **Not confirmed in these frames.**  \nThere are no frames showing musical performances, dance numbers, or live entertainment acts. While the global summary mentions Lady Gaga and John Legend, none of these 30 frames depict such performances.\n\n---\n\n### 6) **Behind-the-scenes moments**\n✅ **Partially confirmed.**  \nFrames 4–8 show a woman wearing headphones with on-screen text reading “‘A SISTER’ DELPHINE GIRARD” — likely a nominee or short film credit. Frame 8 also shows a production schedule board, suggesting a behind-the-scenes or nominee feature segment. This is not a “full” behind-the-scenes look but does include non-stage moments.\n\n---\n\n### **Crucial: Evidence specific to the 2020 Oscars?**\n\n⚠️ **NO CLEAR YEAR INDICATOR.**\n\nWhile the events depicted — particularly Bong Joon-ho’s win for *Parasite* (Frames 1–3, 26–30) and Peter Farrelly’s win for *Green Book* (Frames 15, 18–25) — are **historically associated with the 2019 Oscars (held in February 2019)**, not the 2020 Oscars (held in February 2020).\n\n- **Bong Joon-ho won Best Director and Best Picture for *Parasite* at the 92nd Academy Awards (2020 ceremony, honoring 2019 films)** — so technically, this *is* the 2020 Oscars.\n- **Peter Farrelly won Best Picture for *Green Book* at the 91st Academy Awards (2019 ceremony, honoring 2018 films)** — this is **NOT** the 2020 Oscars.\n\n➡️ **Contradiction:** The video includes footage from **at least two different Oscars ceremonies** — the 2019 ceremony (Farrelly) and the 2020 ceremony (Bong Joon-ho). This directly contradicts the claim that the video narrates “the complete process of the 2020 Oscars ceremony.”\n\nAdditionally:\n- No on-screen text, logo, or graphic explicitly says “2020 Oscars.”\n- The ABC logo appears in many frames — consistent with the network that broadcasts the Oscars — but does not specify the year.\n- The stage design in Frame 9 (“LIVE ACTION SHORT FILM”) matches the 2020 Oscars stage, but the inclusion of 2019 winners (Farrelly) proves this is a **compilation**, not a single ceremony.\n\n---\n\n### ✅ **Conclusion:**\n\nThe video **does not** narrate the complete process of the **2020 Oscars ceremony**. It is a **compilation** that includes moments from **multiple Oscars ceremonies**, including at least the **2019 (91st)** and **2020 (92nd)** Academy Awards.\n\n**Therefore, answer '1' is INCORRECT.**\n\n--- \n\n**Final Answer: ❌ No, the video do